      "text": "                                                                CONSISTENT HASHING\nThe Problem It Solves:\n\nRegular hash-based sharding uses shard = hash(key) % N. When N changes (adding or removing a shard), almost every key maps to a different \nshard.\n\nThe math:\n\n4 shards: hash(key) % 4. Key with hash 100 → shard 0.\nAdd 1 shard (now 5): hash(key) % 5. Key with hash 100 → shard 0. BUT hash 101 → was shard 1, now shard 1. Hash 104 → was shard 0, now \nshard 4.\n\nFor random hashes, approximately (N-1)/N fraction of all keys change shards when going from N to N+1 shards.\nWith 10 shards, adding 1 reshuffles ~90% of keys.\n\nFor a database, this means moving 90% of your data. For a cache, this means 90% cache miss rate during the transition — everyone goes to \nthe database simultaneously. This is called a CACHE AVALANCHE.\n\n                                                        THE RING - HOW CONSISTENT HASHING WORKS\n\nImagine all possible hash values arranged in a circle. The range is 0 to 2³² (about 4.3 billion for 32-bit hashes). This circle is called the hash ring.\n\nBoth servers (nodes) and keys are mapped onto this ring by hashing them:\n\n              0 (= 2^32)\n                  │\n        7.     1. │\n              ─── Node A (hash: 50)\n          .            .\n        6.                .\n                            .\n        5.               Node B (hash: 150)\n          .            .\n        4.                 .  3.\n                            .\n                         Node C (hash: 280)\n\nNumbers represent clock positions (not actual values). The ring goes from 0 clockwise back to 0.\n\nTo find which node stores a key:\nHash the key to find its position on the ring. Walk clockwise from that position until you hit a node. That node is responsible for this key.\n\nKey \"user_123\" → hash → position 90 → walk clockwise → hit Node B (at 150)\nKey \"user_456\" → hash → position 200 → walk clockwise → hit Node C (at 280)\nKey \"user_789\" → hash → position 300 → walk clockwise → → → → hit Node A (at 50, wrapping around)\n                                           ADDING OR REMOVING NODES - WHY ONLY 1/N KEYS MOVE\n\nAdding Node D at position 220:\n\n        Before:                                   After:\nKey at position 200 → Node C    Key at position 200 → Node D\nKey at position 210 → Node C    Key at position 210 → Node D\nKey at position 230 → Node C    Key at position 230 → Node C (unchanged)\nKey at position 250 → Node C    Key at position 250 → Node C (unchanged)\n\nOnly keys between Node B (150) and Node D (220) need to move — they were going to Node C, now they go to Node D. Everything between \nNode D and Node C (220-280) stays on Node C. Everything else is completely unaffected.\n\nWith N nodes, adding one new node moves approximately 1/N of keys. With 10 nodes, adding an 11th moves ~9% of keys. Compare to regular \nhashing where it moves ~90%.\n\nRemoving Node B (at position 150):\n\nNode B's keys were all keys from Node A's position (50) to Node B's position (150). These now fall to Node C (the next clockwise). Only those \nkeys move. Everything else is unaffected.\n\nVirtual Nodes — Solving Uneven Distribution\n\nWith real hash functions, random node positions on the ring won't distribute evenly. You might get:\n\nNode A: covers 10% of the ring\nNode B: covers 60% of the ring\nNode C: covers 30% of the ring\n\nNode B handles 6x more keys than Node A. Uneven load.\n\nThe solution: virtual nodes (vnodes). Each physical server maps to MULTIPLE positions on the ring (100-200 virtual nodes each).\n\nNode A (Physical) → Virtual positions: 50, 120, 310, 890, 1450...\nNode B (Physical) → Virtual positions: 80, 200, 400, 700, 1200...\nNode C (Physical) → Virtual positions: 30, 150, 350, 600, 1100...\n\nWith many virtual nodes per physical server, the distribution becomes statistically even — the law of large numbers ensures each physical server \ngets roughly 1/N of the data.\n\nWhen a node fails:\nWithout virtual nodes: Node B fails → all its load dumps onto Node C (its neighbor) → Node C gets crushed.\n\nWith virtual nodes: Node B fails → its 200 virtual positions are distributed across all other nodes → load spreads evenly across all survivors.\n\nReal-World Usage\n\nApache Cassandra: Uses consistent hashing with virtual nodes as its core data distribution mechanism. This is why Cassandra can add or re-\nmove nodes with minimal data movement.\n\nAmazon DynamoDB: Uses consistent hashing internally for distributing data across storage nodes.\n\nRedis Cluster: Divides the hash space into 16,384 hash slots. Each master node is responsible for a range of slots. Adding a node migrates a \nsubset of slots from existing nodes. This is consistent hashing with a fixed granularity.\n\nCDN Content Routing: Consistent hashing decides which CDN edge server caches which content. Adding a new edge server doesn't invalidate the\nentire cache — only a proportional fraction of content moves.\n\nNginx upstream hashing: Consistent hash load balancing (hash $request_uri consistent) routes the same URL to the same backend server, \nenabling effective application-level caching.\n\n                                                        THE CONNECTION TO SHARDING\n\nConsistent hashing is how you implement hash-based sharding in a system where the number of shards changes. Without consistent hashing, \nadding a shard means moving almost all your data. With consistent hashing, adding a shard means moving only 1/N of your data.\n\nThe relationship:\n\nSharding = the decision to split data across multiple nodes\nConsistent Hashing = the algorithm that determines WHICH node each piece of data goes to,\n                           in a way that minimizes data movement when nodes are added/removed",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                                CONSISTENT HASHING\nThe Problem It Solves:\n\nRegular hash-based sharding uses shard = hash(key) % N. When N changes (adding or removing a shard), almost every key maps to a different \nshard.\n\nThe math:\n\n4 shards: hash(key) % 4. Key with hash 100 → shard 0.\nAdd 1 shard (now 5): hash(key) % 5. Key with hash 100 → shard 0. BUT hash 101 → was shard 1, now shard 1. Hash 104 → was shard 0, now \nshard 4.\n\nFor random hashes, approximately (N-1)/N fraction of all keys change shards when going from N to N+1 shards.\nWith 10 shards, adding 1 reshuffles ~90% of keys.\n\nFor a database, this means moving 90% of your data. For a cache, this means 90% cache miss rate during the transition — everyone goes to \nthe database simultaneously. This is called a CACHE AVALANCHE.\n\n                                                        THE RING - HOW CONSISTENT HASHING WORKS\n\nImagine all possible hash values arranged in a circle. The range is 0 to 2³² (about 4.3 billion for 32-bit hashes). This circle is called the hash ring.\n\nBoth servers (nodes) and keys are mapped onto this ring by hashing them:\n\n              0 (= 2^32)\n                  │\n        7.     1. │\n              ─── Node A (hash: 50)\n          .            .\n        6.                .\n                            .\n        5.               Node B (hash: 150)\n          .            .\n        4.                 .  3.\n                            .\n                         Node C (hash: 280)\n\nNumbers represent clock positions (not actual values). The ring goes from 0 clockwise back to 0.\n\nTo find which node stores a key:\nHash the key to find its position on the ring. Walk clockwise from that position until you hit a node. That node is responsible for this key.\n\nKey \"user_123\" → hash → position 90 → walk clockwise → hit Node B (at 150)\nKey \"user_456\" → hash → position 200 → walk clockwise → hit Node C (at 280)\nKey \"user_789\" → hash → position 300 → walk clockwise → → → → hit Node A (at 50, wrapping around)\n                                           ADDING OR REMOVING NODES - WHY ONLY 1/N KEYS MOVE\n\nAdding Node D at position 220:\n\n        Before:                                   After:\nKey at position 200 → Node C    Key at position 200 → Node D\nKey at position 210 → Node C    Key at position 210 → Node D\nKey at position 230 → Node C    Key at position 230 → Node C (unchanged)\nKey at position 250 → Node C    Key at position 250 → Node C (unchanged)\n\nOnly keys between Node B (150) and Node D (220) need to move — they were going to Node C, now they go to Node D. Everything between \nNode D and Node C (220-280) stays on Node C. Everything else is completely unaffected.\n\nWith N nodes, adding one new node moves approximately 1/N of keys. With 10 nodes, adding an 11th moves ~9% of keys. Compare to regular \nhashing where it moves ~90%.\n\nRemoving Node B (at position 150):\n\nNode B's keys were all keys from Node A's position (50) to Node B's position (150). These now fall to Node C (the next clockwise). Only those \nkeys move. Everything else is unaffected.\n\nVirtual Nodes — Solving Uneven Distribution\n\nWith real hash functions, random node positions on the ring won't distribute evenly. You might get:\n\nNode A: covers 10% of the ring\nNode B: covers 60% of the ring\nNode C: covers 30% of the ring\n\nNode B handles 6x more keys than Node A. Uneven load.\n\nThe solution: virtual nodes (vnodes). Each physical server maps to MULTIPLE positions on the ring (100-200 virtual nodes each).\n\nNode A (Physical) → Virtual positions: 50, 120, 310, 890, 1450...\nNode B (Physical) → Virtual positions: 80, 200, 400, 700, 1200...\nNode C (Physical) → Virtual positions: 30, 150, 350, 600, 1100...\n\nWith many virtual nodes per physical server, the distribution becomes statistically even — the law of large numbers ensures each physical server \ngets roughly 1/N of the data.\n\nWhen a node fails:\nWithout virtual nodes: Node B fails → all its load dumps onto Node C (its neighbor) → Node C gets crushed.\n\nWith virtual nodes: Node B fails → its 200 virtual positions are distributed across all other nodes → load spreads evenly across all survivors.\n\nReal-World Usage\n\nApache Cassandra: Uses consistent hashing with virtual nodes as its core data distribution mechanism. This is why Cassandra can add or re-\nmove nodes with minimal data movement.\n\nAmazon DynamoDB: Uses consistent hashing internally for distributing data across storage nodes.\n\nRedis Cluster: Divides the hash space into 16,384 hash slots. Each master node is responsible for a range of slots. Adding a node migrates a \nsubset of slots from existing nodes. This is consistent hashing with a fixed granularity.\n\nCDN Content Routing: Consistent hashing decides which CDN edge server caches which content. Adding a new edge server doesn't invalidate the\nentire cache — only a proportional fraction of content moves.\n\nNginx upstream hashing: Consistent hash load balancing (hash $request_uri consistent) routes the same URL to the same backend server, \nenabling effective application-level caching.\n\n                                                        THE CONNECTION TO SHARDING\n\nConsistent hashing is how you implement hash-based sharding in a system where the number of shards changes. Without consistent hashing, \nadding a shard means moving almost all your data. With consistent hashing, adding a shard means moving only 1/N of your data.\n\nThe relationship:\n\nSharding = the decision to split data across multiple nodes\nConsistent Hashing = the algorithm that determines WHICH node each piece of data goes to,\n                           in a way that minimizes data movement when nodes are added/removed",
